#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176083 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3fcd243-71c6-56d2-9b1b-700f2cd11ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176084 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aa725153-856c-511e-8bbe-7a63286c2994" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Decay Model"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176085 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ea69de7-f96b-5949-9aec-584cb4e2f214" :knowledge-point/title "Computing an Unknown Time Given an Exponential Growth or Decay Model With an Unknown Rate"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176086 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4aeeaf85-0cbd-58ab-91d6-93448ff54b4d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Initial Value"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176959 :tutorial/content "If a population is growing, we are often interested to know how long it will take for the population to reach a particular size. \n\nSince the rate of growth of a population is often modeled using a differential equation, we typically follow a three-step process:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the general solution of the differential equation.\n\n- **Step 2:** Apply an initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\n- **Step 3:** Use the particular solution to set up and solve an equation for the unknown quantity.\n\nLet's apply this process to an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "14761f37-3844-586b-b7ff-2a4f9fd24137" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14658 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176960 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the population $P(t)$ of a particular bacteria grows according to the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac15 P,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in hours. There were initially $1\\,000$ bacteria. How long will it take for the population size to increase to $100\\,000?$\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the general solution.\n\nThe given differential equation is an exponential growth equation. The general solution is\n\n$$\nP(t) = P_0 e^{t/5},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_0$ is the initial population.\n\n- **Step 2:** Find the particular solution.\n\nWe are given that the initial population is $1\\,000.$ Therefore, we set $P_0 = 1\\,000$ to get the particular solution:\n\n$$\nP(t) = 1\\,000 e^{t/5}\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Solve for the required value.\n\nTo find how long it takes for the population to reach $100\\,000,$ we set $P(t)=100\\,000$ and solve for $t{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100\\,000 & =1\\,000\\,{e}^{t/5} \\\\ \\frac{100\\,000}{1\\,000} & ={e}^{t/5} \\\\ 100 & ={e}^{t/5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}100 & =\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{t/5}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}{10}^{2} & =\\frac{t}{5} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{ln}10 & =\\frac{t}{5} \\\\ t & =10\\operatorname{ln}10 \\\\ t & \\approx 23\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, it takes approximately $23$ hours for the bacteria population to grow to $100\\,000.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4cc9a909-30ea-54a1-a15b-2d50cd72f997" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14907 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"}]}
